    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "9939b4a5-f631-322f-9bd6-4c5a2bfcd443",
                "word": "dyothelism",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "20113631-bbb4-30ad-b91b-a3b175636763",
                        "entry_id": "9939b4a5-f631-322f-9bd6-4c5a2bfcd443",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The doctrine of two wills (human and divine) in Christ."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "42110c4e-f600-4543-986b-c4aa05358d01",
                                "sense_id": "20113631-bbb4-30ad-b91b-a3b175636763",
                                "text": "From that day, dyothelism became the official doctrine of the Orthodox Church, both in the East and in the West; and in the eighth century it found a most subtle expounder in John of Damascus."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "760884fc-2e34-433d-9d45-dea2a13831d4",
                                "sense_id": "20113631-bbb4-30ad-b91b-a3b175636763",
                                "text": "Two years later Anastasius II reinstituted dyothelism, and the same bishops who had two years before vetoed dyothelism now changed their mind, and adopted it as the only true exposition of faith !"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f3ca6223-4864-48d4-8b51-54dd258c622d",
                                "sense_id": "20113631-bbb4-30ad-b91b-a3b175636763",
                                "text": "Finally, the concept of divine adoption becomes a controlling feature of the theology of deification in Maximus, due to his concern to maintain dyothelism in christology, as well as in ascetic anthropology."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
